<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n106988" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὑπά̆τ-η</span></b>  (sc. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">χορδή</span>), ἡ, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>the highest of the three strings</i> which
			formed the framework of the musical scale (opp. <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">νεάτη, μέση</span>), but <i>the lowest</i>
			in pitch, <i><span class="en">
				Philol.
				6
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Pl.
				<u>R.</u>
				443d
			</span></i>, etc.; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">αἱ ὑπάται</span>”
				<i>the highest tetrachord,</i>
				 <i><span class="en">
					Anon.
					<u>Oxy.</u>
					667.16
				</span></i>
			; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τὴν ἀπὸ τῶν ὑπάτων . .
				ἐπίτασιν</span> raising of pitch from the <i>low notes,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Antyll.
			</span></i> ap. <i><span class="en">
				Orib.
				6.10.7
			</span></i>; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">τὸ βομβυκέστερον τῶν ὑπατῶν</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Nicom.
					<u>Harm.</u>
					11
				</span></i>
			; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὑπάτη ὑπατῶν</span> ibid. (but 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὑ. ὑπάτων</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Cleonid.
					<u>Harm.</u>
					4
				</span></i>
			).</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
